diff --git slices/grounding/math/module.ttl slices/grounding/math/module.ttl
index 86cc22a8b..ffc86d750 100644
--- slices/grounding/math/module.ttl
+++ slices/grounding/math/module.ttl
@@ -6164,6 +6164,11 @@ math:InnerProductSpace
     rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/math> ;
     rdfs:label "Inner Product Space"@x-gmeow-english ;
     skos:definition "A vector space equipped with an inner product — a math:VectorSpace carrying a math:innerProduct that assigns lengths and angles, so orthogonality and orthogonal complements are defined. A subclass of math:VectorSpace; the complex case carries a math:HermitianInnerProduct. It names the inner product it carries through math:innerProduct."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    skos:scopeNote "The real case uses a symmetric positive-definite bilinear form; the complex case uses a conjugate-symmetric positive-definite sesquilinear form. Zero dimension, finite and infinite dimension are allowed. Completeness is an additional Hilbert-space condition, not a consequence of this type."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    skos:example "On real R², h(u,v) = u1 v1 + 2 u2 v2 is an inner product with h(v,v) = v1² + 2 v2², positive for every nonzero v. The coordinate formula presupposes the supplied ordered basis and real scalar interpretation."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    gmeow:useWhen "Use when a vector space carries a justified inner product needed for lengths, norms or orthogonality. Retain the space, carrier set, scalar field, coordinate basis and form as separate roles."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not infer an inner product from a vector-space, norm or symmetric-matrix label alone, or treat positive-semidefinite or indefinite forms as positive-definite. An angle involving a zero vector is undefined, and complex angle conventions must be stated."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    gmeow:howToUse "Describe the vector-space structure and link its supplied form with math:innerProduct. Keep the inherited carrier, operation and law evidence explicit. A class or form link proves neither the inner-product laws nor completeness and does not invoke the finite real rational numeric core."@x-gmeow-english ;
     gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .
 
 math:HermitianInnerProduct
@@ -6172,6 +6177,11 @@ math:HermitianInnerProduct
     rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/math> ;
     rdfs:label "Hermitian Inner Product"@x-gmeow-english ;
     skos:definition "A conjugate-symmetric, positive-definite sesquilinear form on a complex vector space — the inner product ⟨·,·⟩ of the complex case, linear in one argument and conjugate-linear in the other. A subclass of math:MathematicalObject carried by a math:InnerProductSpace through math:innerProduct; it is the structure that defines a math:OrthogonalComplement in the complex (S⊥) case (math:definedByInnerProduct)."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    skos:scopeNote "Preserve which argument is conjugate-linear. Under the first-conjugate convention, h(au,bv) = conjugate(a) b h(u,v) and h(v,u) = conjugate(h(u,v)). Positive definiteness requires real h(v,v) > 0 for nonzero v, with h(0,0) = 0."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    skos:example "On C with h(z,w) = conjugate(z) w, h(i,1) = -i, h(1,i) = i and h(i,i) = 1. This form is conjugate-linear in the first argument and linear in the second; it is not complex bilinear."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a justified positive-definite Hermitian inner product on a complex vector space, retaining its actual scalar field, argument convention and coordinate representation independently."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not identify complex sesquilinearity with bilinearity or ordinary symmetry. A Hermitian matrix can be indefinite or positive semidefinite; its label or conjugate symmetry alone does not establish an inner product."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    gmeow:howToUse "Name the form as its own math:HermitianInnerProduct resource. Use math:innerProduct from its space and math:definedByInnerProduct from a complement where applicable. Retain evidence for the laws; these declarations do not execute a complex Hermitian calculation or construct a complement."@x-gmeow-english ;
     gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .
 
 math:Subspace
@@ -6179,7 +6189,12 @@ math:Subspace
     rdfs:subClassOf math:VectorSpace ;
     rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/math> ;
     rdfs:label "Subspace"@x-gmeow-english ;
-    skos:definition "A vector subspace: a subset of a vector space closed under addition and scalar multiplication, itself a math:VectorSpace. A subclass of math:VectorSpace naming the space it sits in through math:subspaceOf. The embedded image of an embedding and the residual of a projection are subspaces; a math:OrthogonalComplement is the subspace orthogonal to one."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    skos:definition "A vector subspace: a subset of a vector space closed under addition and scalar multiplication, itself a math:VectorSpace. A subclass of math:VectorSpace naming the space it sits in through math:subspaceOf. The image of a linear map is a subspace; an arbitrary embedding image or projection residual needs separate subspace justification. A math:OrthogonalComplement is the subspace orthogonal to an operand subspace within a declared inner-product space."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    skos:scopeNote "For W to be a subspace of V over F, W contains zero and uses the induced addition and scalar action of V, closed under every scalar in the same field F. Zero and whole-space subspaces, finite and infinite dimension are allowed. Algebraic closure here does not require topological closedness."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    skos:example "In the real vector space R³ with its usual operations, W = {(x,y,0) : x,y in R} is a subspace, including zero and closed under real linear combinations. The affine plane {(x,y,1) : x,y in R} is not a subspace because it lacks zero."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    gmeow:useWhen "Use for a justified vector subspace with its ambient vector-space structure and common scalar field retained. A linear-map image or a supplied span can establish a subspace; sampled vectors alone do not establish closure of the sample set."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    gmeow:avoidWhen "Do not infer a subspace from an embedding label, a dimension, an arbitrary nonlinear image or residual data. An orthogonal complement requires the declared ambient inner product; a global orthogonal direct-sum decomposition needs its own hypotheses in infinite dimension."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    gmeow:howToUse "Describe W as math:Subspace and link each justified ambient V with math:subspaceOf. Retain math:underlyingSet, math:structureOperation and math:satisfiesAxiom evidence separately from descriptive math:scalarField links. A type or ambient link supplies no automatic closure proof or subspace computation."@x-gmeow-english ;
     gmeow:graphBoxRole gmeow:boxTBox .
 
 math:OrthogonalComplement
@@ -6200,7 +6215,12 @@ math:Basis
     rdfs:subClassOf math:MathematicalObject ;
     rdfs:isDefinedBy <https://blackcatinformatics.ca/gmeow/slices/math> ;
     rdfs:label "Basis"@x-gmeow-english ;
-    skos:definition "A basis of a vector space: a linearly independent set that spans it, so every vector has unique coordinates. A subclass of math:MathematicalObject named by a space through math:hasBasis. An orthonormal basis is the eigenvector set of a self-adjoint operator; a math:PrincipalComponent set is the basis PCA selects."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    skos:definition "An algebraic basis of a vector space over its scalar field: a linearly independent spanning family. Every vector has unique finitely supported coefficients indexed by the basis elements. The basis is distinct from an ordering of its elements, a coordinate array and an inner product."@x-gmeow-english ;
+    skos:scopeNote "Uniqueness is relative to the vector space and scalar field. Indexed coefficients need not form an ordered tuple; positional coordinates additionally require a correspondence between positions and basis elements. A named Basis record alone does not establish independence or spanning."@x-gmeow-english ;
